    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build succeeds and all tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`). The full compile also exercised the OpenAPI code generation and MapStruct processing, so the new `householdId` field and mapper wiring compile cleanly.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `sharesHousehold`/`householdId` feature was already partially scaffolded \u2014 the request flag, `DuplicateHouseholdException.rejectIfDuplicate` (which already *allows* a shared-household owner past the duplicate check), and the OpenAPI request field all existed. The only missing piece was returning a stable, shared `householdId`.\n\nI implemented it as a **derived value**, mirroring the repo's existing pattern (`CustomerCode` util, `displayName`/`initials` mapper expressions), rather than adding persistence:\n\n1. **`util/Household.java`** (new, small cohesive class) \u2014 `idFor(lastName, address)` derives a stable id from the normalized `lastName|address` (same normalization rule the duplicate check uses: case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsed) via a SHA-256 hex prefix.\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 one `@Mapping` expression on `toOwnerDto` that populates `householdId` from the owner's last name + address.\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `householdId` property to the `Owner` response schema so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n\nBecause the id is derived deterministically from last name + address, any two owners in the same household resolve to the **same** identifier automatically \u2014 no schema/DB migration, no entity field, no changes to the large controller method or the exception class. Structural impact is confined to one new tiny class plus a single annotation line on one mapper method.",
